From 60104465021b5791de89e6c2b9d718fc84161f29 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lucascouts <lucascs@protonmail.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 12:22:50 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] agent_ui, sidebar: a thread carries a description
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Zeo next proposal §6.6 (ask 19). A thread had a title and nothing to say
what it is for. It gains a description: one line, up to 280 characters,
edited from "Edit Description…" in the agent menu's Current Thread
section and in the thread's menu in the threads sidebar, beside "Tags…".
The modal is prefilled with the current text; Enter saves, a blank line
clears it, Escape keeps it.

It shows as an Info icon whose tooltip reads it: in the agent panel's
toolbar right after the title view, and after the title on the thread's
sidebar row (ThreadItem's badge). The proposal asked for a subtitle in
the header; the toolbar is one line high, so the icon carries it instead.
The icon sits beside the title view, not inside it: on an opaque window
the title view lays a fade over its right end, and the first version,
drawn inside, was covered by it. A GPUI test cannot see that (its window
is not opaque); the Xvfb proof did. The sidebar's badge is placed below
0063's devtools badge in the builder chain, out of that hunk's context.

Stored in a domain of its own, ThreadDescriptionDb, not as a column of
0048's thread_organization as the proposal planned. That table's
migrations are a list two patches append to -- 0063 adds devtools_root
under the devtools-panel USE flag -- and a step appended by an earlier
patch lands at a different index with and without that flag, which
sqlez refuses on the next start. A domain of its own has its own list,
whatever the flags. The rules are the organization's: a draft is
refused (admit), and a deleted thread's description goes with it
(thread_organization::forget calls description::forget). Writes go
through one queued writer; a failed load keeps the session in memory.

Tests: agent_panel::tests::description_header_tests (the icon appears
beside the title once a description is set, and the panel reads it);
thread_organization::description::tests (whitespace collapsed,
blank clears, the 280-character cut; its own migration list, which no
thread_organization step mentions; a saved thread's description
survives a restart and a blank one is cleared on disk; a draft is
refused; a deleted thread's is forgotten, on disk too; the modal is
prefilled, saves, and clears). The restart and modal tests were seen
failing with set_description made a no-op; the forget test passed under
that probe, so it now checks its precondition first.
---

